Instructions::
1. Configure general router configurations on all routers. All password must me "pass".
2. Configure all Router IP Add as per network diagram
3. Configure EIGRP on E1, E2 and E3. Use AS #10. Advertsie all directly connected networks on E2 and E3. 
 On E1, only advertise f0/0's network, do not advertise f0/1's network on E1.
4. Verify EIGRP connectivity from E3
---> E3 must be able to ping all E2's loopbacks
---> E3 must not be able to ping this address: 185.32.40.202
(If you were able to ping, remove the network advertisement for the 185.32.40.200 network on E1's EIGRP config
5. Configure default routing on E1 pointing to the ISP. Use exit interface on this configuration.
6. Test the deault route connectivity. E1 should be able to ping any of the ISP's loopback ip addresses.
7. Configure static routing on ISP1 routing all traffic for the 185.32.40.80/29 network towards E1. Use the neighbor next hop ip address for this configuration.
8. Configure NAT on E1. It should translate all its internal private network to this Public network range 185.32.40.80 /29.
9. Verify the NAT translation 
---> Do an extended  ping from E1 with the ff. parameters:
     SIP: 192.168.100.1
     DIP: 50.0.0.1
    (This should be succesfully ping)
---> Issue this command: 
    #sh ip nat translation (your router should have entries for its translation table, 
translating the Inside Local Address 192.168.100.1 to an Inside GLobal address that 
is within the usable IP Address range fo the 185.32.40.80/29 
